Cris and neural dissociation syndrome:

Yes. Show it as a hint in the opening scene, not explained, not labeled. The reader notices something slightly off in how he processes the moment — a fractional delay between what he sees and how he reacts, a moment where his eyes track something that isn't there, one line of internal monologue that doesn't quite connect to what's happening around him. No diagnosis. No explanation. Just the grain of something wrong that the reader files away and doesn't understand until later.

What causes it — years of diving into other people's distortions as a hunter. He's absorbed too many fragments from too many hollowed victims. His neural architecture has been repeatedly stressed by processing emotional residue that doesn't belong to him. The syndrome is the accumulated cost. He functions. He's effective. But the edges are fraying in ways Tomas notices and doesn't mention and Cris knows Tomas notices.

