Appearance, voice, accent — per character:

Kai — medium build that reads as larger because of how he occupies space. Scarring across his left forearm and collarbone from the wars. Dark hair kept short. Mexican features, light brown skin. His voice is quiet by default — the kind of quiet that comes from someone who learned early that raising your voice is a last resort. Spanish-accented English that softens almost entirely when he speaks to Alma. When angry he goes quieter, not louder.

Kieran — same bone structure as Kai but worn differently. Leaner. The arena years carved everything unnecessary away. Eyes that assess everything as a variable. He speaks in complete sentences always — no trailing thoughts, no half-finished ideas. The accent they share from Tepito is almost entirely suppressed in him, deliberate, a choice he made early about who he wanted to be read as.

Alma — Kai's coloring, Luz's cheekbones. Prosopagnosia means she looks at people slightly differently — not at the face but just past it, at the whole person. Most people can't articulate what's slightly unusual about how she looks at them. She knows they notice. She's used to it. Her voice is warm but direct — she says what she means because she's never had the social facial cues to learn the art of saying something else.

Luz/Makoto — in her synthetic body she's been rebuilt for function. Military-grade frame, war-based armory. But the face they gave her is designed to be forgettable — a face that doesn't draw attention. Her original appearance is something Kai remembers and the reader won't see until a specific moment. Her voice in the Makoto state is flat with professional precision. Underneath, in fragments that surface, it's something else entirely.

Mara — tall for a Romanian woman, which she uses deliberately. Sophisticated chrome visible at the jaw line and one forearm — not hidden, not displayed, just present. When the synesthesia breaks through her neutralizer her eyes change — pupils dilating at sounds, a slight wince at certain frequencies. Her accent is Romanian-inflected English that gets thicker when she's tired or angry. She speaks slowly when she wants to be understood and very fast when she doesn't.

Tomas — the kind of young man who looks younger than he is because hardship hasn't fully settled into his features yet. Crale street kid build — lean, quick, energy in his hands. He gestures constantly when he talks. His voice is still settling into itself. He laughs at wrong moments, not because he finds things funny but because his nervous system hasn't learned another response to certain kinds of pressure yet.

Cris — was once the kind of man who filled a courtroom. Now he looks like a building after an earthquake — the structure is still there, you can see what it was, but something has shifted that can't be shifted back. Silver at his temples. His suit is expensive and maintained meticulously — the last visible connection to what he was. South American-inflected English that becomes more pronounced when he's tired. He speaks in prosecutor's cadences still — precise, structured, building toward a point.

Ashira — Japanese precision in everything. Compact, still, the kind of still that reads as watchful rather than passive. Chrome is minimal and specific — nothing decorative, everything functional. Her voice is low, speaks less than everyone around her, each sentence complete. Japanese-accented English that she's made no effort to soften because softening it was never the point.

Elena — looks like someone who lives inside her own head and has arranged her appearance to require the minimum possible attention from others. Practical everything. The old hardware she carries is more interesting to look at than she tries to be. Her voice is precise and slightly too fast — she's thinking faster than she speaks and editing on the fly. No strong accent, she's moved too many times and absorbed too many places.

Now — unique chrome per character:

This is where we differentiate fully from CP2077. Each piece should serve the character's specific story, not just their combat function.

KAI — The Surge plus:
Beyond the Surge installed without his knowledge, Kai has standard merc chrome — Lucent optics, Crux in his spine, subdermal reinforcement across his torso from the wars. One piece that's specifically his: Pressure Hands — not a weapon, a tool. His palms and fingertips have pressure-sensitivity chrome that lets him feel structural integrity through touch. He uses it to assess buildings, vehicles, equipment — an old habit from the wars when knowing whether a wall would hold mattered more than anything. It has no combat application. He's never upgraded it because nobody has made him feel like he should. It's the piece of chrome that tells you who he was before all the rest.

KIERAN — Combat-focused, council-appropriate:
He's built for authority as much as combat. Voice Modulator installed at the larynx — not to disguise his voice but to calibrate it. He can pitch his voice to a frequency that triggers mild discomfort in listeners, or to one that creates subconscious trust. He uses this constantly in council meetings. Nobody knows why certain things he says land differently than when others say the same words. Beyond that — structural chrome throughout his skeleton from the arena years, Ahead installed at the highest grade available, and one piece nobody knows about: a secondary Surge variant installed by whoever is behind the original Surge project. He doesn't know what it is. It hasn't activated yet.

ALMA — Almost none, by design:
Stitch built her synesthesia neutralizer — it runs along her spine and interfaces with her sensory processing chrome in a way that's entirely custom. No one else could have built it and no one else could maintain it. She has standard Lucent optics, slightly modified to not interfere with how she reads non-facial cues. Otherwise she's baseline. In a world where chrome signals status and capability, her near-baseline state makes people underestimate her constantly.

MARA — Sophisticated and specific:
Beyond the synesthesia neutralizer — Resonance Chrome at her fingertips. This is what makes her exceptional at her work rather than just dangerous. The chrome lets her read micro-vibrations through any surface she touches — heartbeat through a table, tension in a floorboard, the tremor of someone's voice in the walls between rooms. She uses it constantly and unconsciously. Combined with her prosopagnosia's-trained reading of movement and habit, she knows things about people's states that she shouldn't be able to know. Combat application: she can feel exactly how tense a person's muscles are before they move. The attack always starts in the body before it reaches the arm.

TOMAS — Almost nothing yet, building:
Standard Crux, basic Lucent optics, the audio chrome that plays his music selections. That's it. He's 21 and hasn't had the money or the contacts for anything more. The chrome he eventually acquires through the story will mark his progression — what he chooses to install and when is a character arc told through augmentation.

CRIS — Specific and worn:
His Trace device is external — he carries it rather than having it installed, deliberate. He doesn't trust installation anymore after what installation did to the people he hunts and the people he's lost. One installed piece: Memory Anchor — chrome at his hippocampus that works against the neural dissociation syndrome. It creates anchor points, specific vivid memories that stay accessible even when the dissociation is fragmenting his timeline. The anchor points he's set: his daughter's voice saying his name. That's the one that stays clearest. When the dissociation gets bad he focuses on that anchor until the timeline settles. The irony that he hunts people who steal memories while using chrome to preserve one specific memory against his own mind's erosion — he's aware of this. He doesn't talk about it.

ELENA — Old and irreplaceable:
Her diving setup is the main chrome — installed at the base of her skull and running forward, interfacing with her visual and auditory processing. It's old enough that modern chrome surgeons wouldn't know how to work on it. Stitch is one of maybe three people alive who understands her specific configuration. She's also got Ghost Fingers — chrome in her hands that lets her interface with old net architecture directly through touch, bypassing standard connection protocols. This is how she accesses the buried net. She has to physically touch specific points in old infrastructure and the chrome reads it. It looks like she's feeling her way through a dark room. Because she is.

DAX — Vehicle-integrated:
His chrome is mostly about driving. Road Sense — installed in his vestibular system, gives him a precise sense of spatial relationship between his vehicle and everything around it in a fifteen-meter radius. He experiences it as extended proprioception — the car is his body. He can feel the road surface through the tires the way you feel the ground through your feet. Beyond that, standard merc chrome. He doesn't put things in his body that don't serve the road.

SILAS — Corporate remnants:
Still running some of the chrome from his corporate years — high-end analytical processing augmentation that makes him exceptional at reading patterns and projecting outcomes. It was installed when he was a corporate law prosecutor and it's the one thing from that life he kept because taking it out would require a surgery he doesn't trust anyone in his current world to perform. He also has Ledger — a memory-storage chrome that records everything he witnesses with legal-standard fidelity. Old habit from prosecution days. He still records everything. He doesn't know yet that this recording will eventually matter enormously.

JOREN — Unpredictable and excessive:
His chrome is the physical expression of his character. Nothing standard, everything customized, several pieces that shouldn't work together but do because someone very skilled and very strange built them at his specific direction. The piece that defines him: Resonant Vocal Chrome — not for modulation like Kieran's, for amplification and frequency manipulation. When Joren wants to be heard he is heard, physically, in the body, in a way that bypasses rational processing and goes straight to the nervous system's alarm response. Combined with his genuine intelligence and his complete unpredictability, he's the most unsettling person in any room he occupies. Kai killing him needs to feel like releasing something that had been building pressure for the entire time we know Joren. Not cathartic. Just finally done.

